      "hypothesis": "Directly executing the 16 seed rounds and initializing the rolling window without a temporary block list removes per-block allocation and traversal.",
      "reason": "The candidate preserves the unchanged universal proof contract and delivers a repeatable, mechanism-backed improvement. Median best Bend CPU time falls from 23.125 ms to 21.375 ms, a 7.57% reduction, exceeding the required 3% threshold.",
      "review": {
        "decision": "approve",
        "reason": "The candidate preserves the unchanged universal proof contract and delivers a repeatable, mechanism-backed improvement. Median best Bend CPU time falls from 23.125 ms to 21.375 ms, a 7.57% reduction, exceeding the required 3% threshold.",
        "evidence": [
          "Only editable files `core.bend` and `conformance.bend` differ from the approved baseline. Protected specifications, public laws, benchmark/validation code, drivers, dependency lock, and trust-contract files are unchanged and their hashes match `benchmarks/proof_contract.json`.",
          "`core.bend` adds `window_compress16`, directly executing the 16 seed rounds and initializing the existing fixed rolling window. `block_bytes` passes its 16 packed words directly, eliminating construction and traversal of the temporary block list while retaining all 64 rounds, feed-forward, and schedule expansion.",
          "The optimization is general and contains no benchmark sizes, seeds, cached digests, external crypto, timer manipulation, skipped work, or harness-specific behavior.",
          "The new unconditional `window_compress16_correct` law quantifies over all 16 words, expansion counts, constant lists, and states. Constant lists of at least 16 elements reduce definitionally to the same seed steps and rolling rounds as `fused_compress`; all shorter-list shapes explicitly fall back to `fused_compress`. No precondition remains to discharge.",
          "`block_bytes_correct` now rewrites `window_compress16` through the new lemma to the former `fused_compress` pipeline, then uses `fused_compress_correct`, `schedule_correct`, and `compression_correct`. Its exhaustive incomplete-tail cases remain intact.",
          "The public proof chain remains connected: `Laws.sha256_correct` invokes `Conformance.hash_correct`, which uses `pad_correct`, `block_bytes_correct`, and `digest_correct` to reach the independent FIPS implementation for arbitrary inputs. Packing masks every input element to its low eight bits; padding, schedule order, modular U32 arithmetic, constants, digest order, and arbitrary lengths remain covered.",
          "All five unchanged public proof implementations remain present. Word correctness feeds `sha256_bytes_correct`; `digest_bytes_correct` proves big-endian serialization recursively; `sha256_bytes_length` proves 32-byte output; and `constants_correct` fixes the FIPS table.",
          "`validation.stdout.log` reports the frozen trust boundary intact, universal and concrete Bend proofs checking, 182 cases passing for each word/byte API on both JS and native backends, rejection of zero-digest, prepended-byte, and reversed-digest mutations, and `RESEARCH VALIDATION PASSED`.",
          "Both frozen drivers invoke the same public `SHA.sha256` proved by `Laws.sha256_correct`. Sequential and parallel CPU measurements are present, parallel runs use `--gpu off`, GPU is excluded, and every digest is checked against hashlib.",
          "All six baseline/candidate reports use schema 3, Bend 2.0.5, seed 2562026, identical corpus hash, five samples per workload, and matched 8 MiB corpora. Calibration retried the complete suite until every final native raw measurement exceeded 20 ms; normalized values equal raw times divided by eight.",
          "Candidate sequential/parallel totals are 114.125/21.375, 113.875/21.625, and 115.0/21.375 ms. Each total equals the sum of its four workload medians, and parallel CPU wins every complete suite. Baseline totals are 125.125/23.625, 124.875/22.875, and 126.25/23.125 ms.",
          "The prescribed candidate median is 21.375 ms versus the approved baseline median of 23.125 ms: a 1.75 ms or 7.57% improvement. Even the slowest candidate report, 21.625 ms, beats the fastest baseline report, 22.875 ms, by 5.46%, placing the gain beyond observed noise.",
          "Architecture-first requirements are satisfied by the inspected implementation and lineage: the approved baseline already uses streaming byte blocks, fused schedule/compression, fixed rotations, and a fixed 16-word rolling schedule. This candidate removes the remaining per-block list allocation and traversal from that architecture rather than choosing an isolated cosmetic tweak. Iteration 1 used the same change and failed only because the orchestrator model was unavailable, not because of a correctness or measurement rejection."
        ],
        "risks": [
          "The 16-round unrolling increases generated code size and may have different performance characteristics on other Bend versions or hardware.",
          "Formal assurance still trusts Bend's checker, Base semantics, and the independent FIPS transcription; compiler/runtime correctness, hardware, and cryptographic security are outside the proof boundary."
        ]
      }
